 ## Background
-Maximum Activity Projection was proposed by Jerold W. Wallis and colleagues in 1990 [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to address the limitations of conventional slice display in nuclear medicine, which is complex to interpret due to the need to examine many slices and follow structures across them. MAP combines the spatial relationships of planar imaging with the high contrast of tomographic slices. It was implemented on a MicroVAX-II and used in clinical SPECT studies for bone scintigraphy and hemangioma detection [S1, unofficial]. The method is distinct from segmentation-based volume rendering as it requires no user-selected parameters or thresholding [S1, unofficial].
